# Copy this file to .env.local for local development (vercel dev / vercel env pull),
# or set these directly in your Vercel project's Settings -> Environment Variables
# for a real deployment. Do not commit a real .env(.local) file — it's already
# ignored by .gitignore.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEXTRIA_API_KEY — the shared secret that gates the shared-backend API
# (api/state.js). Anyone who has this value can read and overwrite the ledger's
# shared data, so treat it like a password: pick something long and random and
# share it only with your team (e.g. via a password manager), not by email.
#
# If this is left unset, the shared backend (once you've added a Redis
# integration — see README.md) is OPEN to anyone who finds the deployment URL.
# That's fine for kicking the tyres, but you should set a real value before
# putting any actual client/matter data behind it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LEXTRIA_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KV_REST_API_URL / KV_REST_API_TOKEN — do NOT set these by hand.
#
# These are injected automatically into your Vercel project once you add a
# Redis integration from the Vercel Marketplace (Storage tab -> Browse Storage
# -> Redis; Upstash for Redis is a good default). Vercel writes them into your
# project's environment variables and redeploys automatically. You only need
# to add the integration once; you never need to copy these values yourself.
#
# Listed here only so their names are documented — leave them blank/unset in
# any .env file you actually create locally, unless you're intentionally
# pointing local dev at a real Redis instance via `vercel env pull`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KV_REST_API_URL=
# KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
